import{_ as o,o as a,c as r,a0 as t}from"./chunks/framework.ChlVJx0I.js";const w=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Tool row headers wrap pi's tool renderers","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"adr/0038-tool-row-headers-wrap-pi-tool-renderers.md","filePath":"adr/0038-tool-row-headers-wrap-pi-tool-renderers.md","lastUpdated":1792135643000}`),s={name:"adr/0038-tool-row-headers-wrap-pi-tool-renderers.md"};function n(i,e,d,l,h,c){return a(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[t('<h1 id="tool-row-headers-wrap-pi-s-tool-renderers" tabindex="-1">Tool row headers wrap pi&#39;s tool renderers <a class="header-anchor" href="#tool-row-headers-wrap-pi-s-tool-renderers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Tool row headers wrap pi&#39;s tool renderers&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Every Tool row sits in the same green block, and the block says nothing about which tool it holds: the <code>bash</code> and <code>powershell</code> rows print only <code>$ &lt;command&gt;</code>, a <code>codemode</code> row prints a JSON blob, and an MCP row prints whatever its server&#39;s renderer chose. So one extension adds a Tool row header - the tool name set in a rule, one line above the call line - by registering a tool-renderer resolver that calls <code>next()</code> first and prepends the header to whatever pi or another extension would have drawn. Wrapping reaches built-in tools, this package&#39;s own tools, and MCP tools through one code path, and it composes with pi&#39;s MCP resolver and with <code>bash_bg</code>&#39;s own renderer instead of shadowing either. The header names the tool and nothing else: the call line already carries the argument, and the block&#39;s background already carries the pending, success, and error states.</p><p><strong>Considered options</strong>: re-registering each built-in tool with new renderers (pi&#39;s own example pattern) covers only the tools named, and it re-registers execution to change a picture; a theme cannot add a line; drawing a full fieldset frame with side rules and a bottom rule needs <code>renderShell: &quot;self&quot;</code>, which hands the extension pi&#39;s whole row shell - background state, the click-to-expand mouse region, inline images, and empty-row hiding - to rebuild and to re-break on every pi change; owning each call line to avoid the repeated tool name inherits pi&#39;s per-tool formatting (read&#39;s compact classification, write&#39;s highlighting, bash&#39;s timers) forever.</p><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Consequences&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>The tool name appears twice in a <code>read</code>, <code>edit</code>, <code>write</code>, <code>grep</code>, <code>find</code>, or <code>ls</code> row: once in the header, once in pi&#39;s call line. This is deliberate. Removing it means parsing pi&#39;s styled output, which pi changes without notice.</li><li>The header is one extra line per row. The block already spends a blank line above and below, and the only zero-cost shape is an inline prefix, which is the owned-call-line design again.</li><li>A <code>bash_bg</code> row is now labeled <code>bash_bg</code>. ADR 0024 still governs the call line: it stays <code>$ &lt;command&gt;</code> with no background marker. The header is a different layer, and it is what finally tells a <code>bash</code> row from a <code>bash_bg</code> row.</li><li>A self-shell row&#39;s header (<code>renderShell: &quot;self&quot;</code>, today only <code>edit</code>) paints the row&#39;s background itself, because pi draws self-shell rows into a plain container that carries no background. It follows the body&#39;s color: pi&#39;s edit box paints its lines from its diff-preview state (green once the diff is computed, red on a preview error, grey until then), and the header reads that same state at paint time, so header and body always share one background. A self-shell tool without that state gets the row&#39;s pending, success, and error colors instead. The header starts one cell in like the shell-box rows&#39; headers.</li><li>The wrapper must keep pi&#39;s inner component in its own state key and hand it back as <code>context.lastComponent</code>, because pi&#39;s built-in renderers call <code>setText</code> on that component and would crash on the wrapper.</li><li>An MCP row&#39;s header drops pi&#39;s <code>mcp__</code> spelling and shows <code>server/tool</code>, the same rule pi&#39;s MCP extension uses for that row&#39;s call line, so the two agree.</li><li>The header draws in HTML export as well as the terminal: the renderer slots carry no mode flag to tell them apart.</li><li>A row whose wrapped renderer draws nothing shows the header alone. No built-in tool does this today.</li><li>The extension is always on with no setting and no command. It is a rendering opinion in a personal package; the way to opt out is to not load the file.</li></ul>',5)])])}const g=o(s,[["render",n]]);export{w as __pageData,g as default};
